;Battlezone (Atari, 1980) - hardware map, RAM variables and equates for
;bzone_program_rom.asm.
;Names are Atari's identifiers from the BZONE.COM module set (MAC65 symbols are
;significant to 6 characters, so a longer spelling is the same symbol as its first 6
;characters; '.' -> '_' and '$' -> 'S_').  A name the source defines with two
;different values keeps the name for the main one; a second ROM/RAM/hardware label
;among the others gets an _ADDR suffix.
;Descriptions are Atari's comments on the definitions (upper case) where the source
;has one; text tagged [note] was written for this disassembly (bit notes from the AAE
;driver ../bzone.cpp, RAM/constant notes from reading the code or, for the COIN65
;cells, from TC65.MAC's own header comments - see define_notes_bzone.txt).  A
;multi-byte cell says its size, from the source's own .BLKB count.
;Generated by disasm/emit_defines.py; every RAM/hardware/constant symbol macasm.py
;found is here, whether or not the program listing itself refers to it by name.

;----------------------------------[ Memory map ]----------------------------------
.alias ZeroPageRam      $00      ;Through $00FF. Control, work, tank/player and display variables.
.alias GameRam          $0100    ;Through $03FF. Object tables, explosions, high scores/initials, logo, volcano-rock arrays (the 6502 stack occupies $0100-$01FF; nothing is named there).
.alias Inputs           $0800    ;IN0 (read): coin switches, self-test, diag step, VG HALT, 3 kHz clock (see Hardware registers).
.alias OptionSw1        $0A00    ;DSW1 option switches (read): coinage / bonus coins.
.alias OptionSw2        $0C00    ;DSW2 option switches (read): lives / missile level / bonus life / language.
.alias CoinCounters     $1000    ;Coin counter / lamp pulses (write, EMCNT).
.alias VgGo             $1200    ;Start the AVG running the display list at vector RAM $2000 (write, GOADD).
.alias Watchdog         $1400    ;Watchdog clear (write, WTCHDG).
.alias VgReset          $1600    ;Reset (halt) the AVG (write, VSTOP).
.alias Mathbox          $1800    ;Status (read $1800), result low/high (read $1810/$1818), registers / function start (write $1860-$187F).
.alias Pokey            $1820    ;Through $182F. POKEY: sound channels 1-4, random number, pot/spinner input, control.
.alias SoundLatch       $1840    ;Discrete-sound latch (write, SOUND).
.alias VectorRam        $2000    ;Through $2FFF. AVG display list, built each frame from VGLIST.
.alias VectorRom        $3000    ;Through $3FFF. Vector shapes/pictures (vector-ROM track, not this file).
.alias Unmapped4000     $4000    ;Through $4FFF. Not decoded by this board revision.
.alias ProgramRom       $5000    ;Through $7FFF (bzone_program_rom.asm); the 6502 vectors are at $7FFA.
.alias RomMirror        $8000    ;Through $FFFF. The 6502 only decodes 15 address bits: an exact mirror of $0000-$7FFF (RAM, hardware and ROM alike); the 6502 reads its vectors here.

;----------------------------------[ Hardware registers ]----------------------------------
.alias S_COINA          $0800    ;[note] Read (also known as $LAM/HALT at this address, IN0): b0 coin 1 (active low), b1 coin 2 (active low), b4 self-test/service mode (active low), b5 diagnostic step (active low).
.alias S_LAM            $0800    ;[note] Same address as $COINA/HALT (IN0); COIN65 reads the slam bit here (see $LMBIT).
.alias HALT             $0800    ;D0=1 FOR HALTED.  [note] Same address as $COINA/$LAM (IN0); bits 6/7 are tested with BIT everywhere in this source: V (bit 6) = VG HALT (the AVG has finished the display list; BSTST.MAC's own comment at the poll loop even says so - "BIT 7 (SIGN FLAG) OF HALT = 3000 HZ SQUARE WAVE", confirming bit 7 too), N (bit 7) = the 3 kHz clock square wave.  Bits 4/5 (self-test, diagnostic step) are instead read with AND (e.g. PWRON: AND #ST_BIT; STEST3: AND #$20).  Atari's own comment on this equate, "D0=1 FOR HALTED", names a different bit to the code's own usage - probably an earlier board revision's documentation that was not updated here.
.alias OPTION           $0A00    ;[note] DSW0/ip_port_1_r (Atari's own schematic numbering calls this bank "DSW1"; MAME's tag is "DSW0"): b0-1 lives, b2-3 missile-appears-at threshold, b4-5 bonus-life schedule (NEWLIF: LSR x4, AND #$03), b6-7 language (MSGS: ROL x4, AND #$06; 00 English, 01 German, 10 French, 11 Spanish; see LNGTBL).
.alias OPTON2           $0C00    ;[note] DSW1/ip_port_2_r (Atari's own schematic numbering calls this bank "DSW2"; MAME's tag is "DSW1"): b0-1 coinage, b2-3 Coin B multiplier, b4 Coin A multiplier, b5-7 bonus-coin schedule; copied to zero-page S_CMODE by the NMI ("C SET COIN MODE") for COIN65 to read.
.alias EMCNT            $1000    ;[note] Write: pulses the 3 electro-mechanical coin counters, one bit per counter from the sign bits of COIN65's $CCTIM timers; the AAE driver leaves this address unhandled (MWA_ROM).
.alias GOADD            $1200    ;RESTART FOR VECTOR GEN.  [note] Write (any value): start the AVG running the display list at vector RAM $2000.
.alias WTCHDG           $1400    ;[note] Write (any value): clear the watchdog timer; must be written periodically (once per NMI, and again while STEST4/RAMTST poll the 3 kHz clock) or the board resets.
.alias VSTOP            $1600    ;[note] Write (any value): reset/halt the AVG; the complement of GOADD.
.alias MBSTAT           $1800    ;[note] Same address as MSTAT; used where the code is reading the busy/status bit rather than the register block base.
.alias MSTAT            $1800    ;[note] Read: Mathbox status, D7 = 1 while busy; also the base address for the rest of the Mathbox register block below (MSTAT+offset: ACOS, BSIN, TPOSXT, ... DST2).  This Mathbox is the same hardware as Tempest's, byte-identical microcode and mapping PROM (see MATHBOX_NOTES.md).
.alias STARTZ           $1800    ;[note] Same numeric value as MSTAT/MBSTAT ($1800), but used by R2D3CK only as a 16-bit constant (STARTZ/100, STARTZ&$FF) to set a newly-spawned buzz bomber's starting Z depth - not as an address.
.alias MOUTLO           $1810    ;[note] Read: Mathbox result, low byte (same address as YLOW) - the generic result byte for whichever function was last started (rotation, divide, distance, ...); also read by BZSTST's MBDIAG self-test.
.alias YLOW             $1810    ;[note] Same address as MOUTLO; used where the value being read is specifically a Y-matrix/rotation result.
.alias MOUTHI           $1818    ;[note] Read: Mathbox result, high byte (same address as YHIGH) - the generic result byte, as MOUTLO.
.alias YHIGH            $1818    ;[note] Same address as MOUTHI; used where the value being read is specifically a Y-matrix/rotation result.
.alias AUDF1            $1820    ;[note] POKEY write: channel 1 frequency (same address as CHAN1F).
.alias CHAN1F           $1820    ;[note] POKEY write: channel 1 frequency; MODSND writes the running sound's current value here.
.alias POKEY            $1820    ;[note] POKEY ($1820-$182F): sound channels 1-4 (frequency/volume pairs), control, random number, pot scan (spinner/cocktail input).
.alias CHAN1V           $1821    ;[note] POKEY write: channel 1 volume/distortion.
.alias CHAN2F           $1822    ;[note] POKEY write: channel 2 frequency.
.alias CHAN2V           $1823    ;[note] POKEY write: channel 2 volume/distortion.
.alias CHAN3F           $1824    ;[note] POKEY write: channel 3 frequency.
.alias CHAN3V           $1825    ;[note] POKEY write: channel 3 volume/distortion.
.alias CHAN4F           $1826    ;[note] POKEY write: channel 4 frequency.
.alias CHAN4V           $1827    ;[note] POKEY write: channel 4 volume/distortion.
.alias ALLPOT           $1828    ;POT INPUT WORD.  [note] POKEY read: pot-scan result byte; this board wires it to the player buttons/switches (BzoneControls / bzone_IN3_r in the AAE driver) - fire, start 1/2, tread switches - not an analogue pot.
.alias AUDCTL           $1828    ;[note] POKEY write: audio control (same address as ALLPOT read).
.alias PRAND            $182A    ;[note] POKEY read: random number, used throughout as the game's dice (enemy AI, sound tables).
.alias POTGO            $182B    ;START POT READ.  [note] POKEY write: start a pot scan (must be struck before reading ALLPOT).
.alias SKCTL            $182F    ;POKEY SCAN KEYBOARD CONTROL.  [note] POKEY write: serial/keyboard control.
.alias SOUND            $1840    ;[note] Write: discrete-sound latch (BzoneSounds in the AAE driver): explosion/shell/engine-rumble trigger bits, decoded outside the 6502 program.
.alias ACOS             $1860    ;[note] Mathbox register block (MSTAT+offset), write-only: the rotation-matrix cosine INPUT - the 6502 writes its own COSXTR-extrapolated cosine table value here (e.g. ROTATE: JSR COSXTR; STA ACOS), not a Mathbox-computed arccosine.
.alias MBOXIN           $1860    ;[note] Same address as ACOS ($1860): start of the Mathbox write-registers block (MAL..MDYPL-style offsets used as MSTAT+n through this source).
.alias BSIN             $1862    ;[note] Mathbox register block, write-only: the rotation-matrix sine INPUT - the 6502 writes its own SINXTR-extrapolated sine table value here (e.g. ROTATE: JSR SINXTR; STA BSIN), not a Mathbox-computed arcsine.
.alias TPOSXT           $1864    ;[note] Mathbox register block: target X position input to the distance/arctangent functions.
.alias TPOSYT           $1866    ;[note] Mathbox register block: target Y position input to the distance/arctangent functions.
.alias XMATRX           $1868    ;[note] Mathbox register block: X rotation-matrix input.
.alias YMATRX           $186A    ;[note] Mathbox register block: Y rotation-matrix input.
.alias YSTRT1           $186B    ;[note] Mathbox register block: Y-matrix-multiply start/strobe (first of two, see YSTRT2).
.alias DIVCYC           $186C    ;[note] Mathbox register block, write-only: written once (LDA #$0A; STA DIVCYC, "C INIT DIVIDER") each time MAIN starts a frame's rotation, to initialize the divider - not a status byte read back by the 6502.
.alias ZLOW             $186D    ;[note] Mathbox register block: Z (depth) divisor, low byte.
.alias ZHI              $186E    ;[note] Mathbox register block: Z (depth) divisor, high byte.
.alias DIVOUT           $186F    ;[note] Mathbox register block, write-only: the divide dividend; the 6502 never reads it back, only MOUTLO/MOUTHI (e.g. TRACK writes the X or Y delta here before STA ZDIV starts the divide).
.alias YSTRT2           $1871    ;[note] Mathbox register block: Y-matrix-multiply start/strobe (second of two, see YSTRT1).
.alias RSTRT1           $1872    ;[note] Mathbox register block: rotation-matrix-multiply start/strobe.
.alias ZDIV             $1874    ;[note] Mathbox register block: generic start-divide strobe - despite the name, not just for the Z divisor (ZLOW/ZHI); TRACK also uses it to start the arctangent division after loading DIVOUT.
.alias XPL              $1875    ;[note] Mathbox register block: divide dividend, low byte.
.alias XPH              $1876    ;[note] Mathbox register block: divide dividend, high byte.
.alias DXP              $1877    ;[note] Mathbox register block: delta-X projection result.
.alias DYPH             $1879    ;[note] Mathbox register block: delta-Y projection result, high byte.
.alias DSTNCE           $187D    ;[note] Mathbox register block: distance-function input/result (collision and radar checks).
.alias DST2             $187E    ;[note] Mathbox register block: second distance-function input/result.

;----------------------------------[ Zero page variables ]----------------------------------
.alias VGSIZE           $00      ;SCALING SIZE
.alias VGBRIT           $01      ;VECTOR BRIGHTNESS
.alias VGLIST           $02      ;VECTOR LIST POINTER  2 bytes.
.alias XCOMP            $04      ;X COMPONENT FOR VECTOR DELTA  4 bytes.
.alias YCOMP            $06      ;Y COMPONENT FOR VECTOR DELTA
.alias TEMP1            $08      ;SCRATCH  2 bytes.
.alias TEMP2            $0A      ;[note] General-purpose scratch word, paired with TEMP1/TEMP3 (used throughout as temporary storage for one routine at a time, not tied to a single purpose)  2 bytes.
.alias TEMP3            $0C      ;[note] General-purpose scratch word, paired with TEMP1/TEMP2  2 bytes.
.alias S_S_CRDT         $0E      ;GAME CREDITS
.alias EXPCNT           $0F      ;EXPLOSION COUNTER
.alias SAVE0            $10      ;SCRATCH  [note] Scratch, reused per-routine rather than tied to one purpose - e.g. DRAW's own INPUT PARAMS comment: "SAVE0=OBJECT # POINTER", its running index into the rotated/translated object table.  2 bytes.
.alias COLFLG           $12      ;COLLISION FLAG  [note] Also indexed as COLFLG,X (X=0 tank, 2 robot - BZONE.MAC:3056); COLFLG+2 lands on SAVE1+1.
.alias SAVE1            $13      ;SCRATCH  [note] Second byte (SAVE1+1) is also reachable as COLFLG+2, the robot's twin of the tank's COLFLG (see COLFLG).  2 bytes.
.alias SNDWRD           $15      ;SOUND WORD  [note] Also indexed as SNDWRD,X (X=0 tank, 2 robot/buzz bomber - SHRTCK/BUZBOM); SNDWRD+2 lands on SAVE2+1.
.alias SAVE2            $16      ;SCRATCH  [note] Second byte (SAVE2+1) is also reachable as SNDWRD+2, the robot's twin of the tank's SNDWRD (see SNDWRD).  2 bytes.
.alias SAVE3            $18      ;WORKING STORAGE FOR ROTAT  [note] Scratch, reused per-routine - e.g. DRAW/PNTPUT's byte offset into the current object's point data ("OBJECT POINT TABLE", inline comment where it's set up).
.alias X1               $19      ;[note] Scratch X coordinate used by M_SET/M_FOR/M_REV (per-tick motion vector), R_37S_, BUZBOM and ROB1 for a temporary point; X2/Y1/Y2 are the rest of the pair(s) (not used by ROTPNT or D_SPCL, despite their own comments naming a similarly-purposed pair)  2 bytes.
.alias X2               $1B      ;[note] Second scratch X coordinate, paired with Y2; also ROTPNT's raw (non-absolute) X/Y-matrix values (see X1)  2 bytes.
.alias Y1               $1D      ;[note] Scratch Y coordinate, pairs with X1 in M_SET/M_FOR/M_REV etc.; also holds ROTPNT's absolute-value Y (ROTPNT_10, compared against X2) (see X1)  2 bytes.
.alias Y2               $1F      ;[note] Second scratch Y coordinate, paired with X2 (see X1)  2 bytes.
.alias S_CNCT           $21      ;COIN COUNT
.alias DDEND            $22      ;INPUT DIVIDEND FOR DIVIDE  2 bytes.
.alias FIRECT           $24      ;COUNTER FOR SHELL TIMER  [note] Also indexed as FIRECT,X (X=0 tank, 2 robot, throughout ROBOT/BUZBOM); FIRECT+2 lands on DQUE+1.
.alias DQUE             $25      ;DEPTH LVE FOR DIMMING OBJECTS  [note] Second byte (DQUE+1) is also reachable as FIRECT+2, the robot's twin of the tank's FIRECT (see FIRECT).  2 bytes.
.alias LANGLE           $27      ;LSB OF 9 BIT ANGLE IN D7  [note] Low bit(s) of the tank/robot's 9-bit heading angle (TANGLE/NTHETA hold the other 8 bits); also indexed as LANGLE,X (X=0 tank, 2 robot).
.alias BUFRDY           $28      ;BUFFER READY FOR DOUBLE BUFFER SYNCH  2 bytes.
.alias TANGLE           $2A      ;TANK ANGLE  [note] Also indexed as TANGLE,X (X=0 tank, 2 robot, throughout ROBOT/TRACK); TANGLE+2 lands on NTHETA+1.
.alias NTHETA           $2B      ;OBJECT'S ORIENTATION, TANK ANGLE  [note] Second byte (NTHETA+1) is also reachable as TANGLE+2, the robot's twin of the tank's TANGLE (see TANGLE).  2 bytes.
.alias TPOSX            $2D      ;TANK'S X POSITION  4 bytes.
.alias TPOSY            $31      ;" Y "  4 bytes.
.alias CURNTX           $35      ;CURRENT BEAN POSITION X  [note] Current vector-beam X position, tracked in RAM so routines that draw a sequence of connected vectors (DOVECT, VOLCNO's mountain-rock draw) can compute each new delta from the beam's last plotted point.  Atari's own comment, "CURRENT BEAN POSITION X", is a typo for "BEAM".  2 bytes.
.alias CURNTY           $37      ;" " Y  [note] Current vector-beam Y position, pairs with CURNTX (see CURNTX; same "BEAN"/BEAM typo on its own comment line).  2 bytes.
.alias SYNC             $39      ;NMI. MAIN LOOP SYNCHRONIZATION
.alias S_INTCT          $3A      ;INTERRUPT COUNTER  [note] Free-running counter incremented once per NMI ("64 MS" in the source comment at the increment); besides timing, its low bits also serve as a slow pseudo-random/staggering source elsewhere (R_EVAD tests S_INTCT&7, R_RAND tests its sign bit).
.alias TBLPTR           $3B      ;POINTER FOR TABLE  2 bytes.
.alias PNTTBL           $3D      ;POINT TABLE  104 bytes.
.alias TRDCTR           $A5      ;[note] Per-object (index 0 tank, 2 robot/buzz bomber, via TRDCTR,X) tread/thrust animation-frame counter, incremented each time that object moves ("C MOVE TREAD", BZONE.MAC:3043); also read as TRDCTR+2 (aliasing into GARBAG+1, see GARBAG below).  (Atari's comment here, "GARBAGE SAVE", belongs to GARBAG below - see there.)
.alias GARBAG           $A6      ;[note] General-purpose 2-byte scratch pair, reused independently by ABSTMP, DRADAR ("RELATIVE ANGLE OF ROBOT") and ROBCHK; its second byte (GARBAG+1) is also reachable as TRDCTR+2 (see TRDCTR above) when a tread-counter call happens to run right before one of these routines reuses it.  (Atari's comment here, "X & Y OF SHELL POSITIONS", belongs to SHELLX/SHELLY below - see there.)  2 bytes.
.alias SHELLX           $A8      ;[note] Shell X position, 4 bytes: tank's shell at SHELLX/+1, robot/buzz-bomber's at SHELLX+2/+3 (indexed SHELLX,X, X=0/2); the comment on GARBAG above, "X & Y OF SHELL POSITIONS", describes this cell and SHELLY  4 bytes.
.alias SHELLY           $AC      ;[note] Shell Y position, 4 bytes: tank/robot pair as SHELLX (see SHELLX)  4 bytes.
.alias SINCX            $B0      ;X & Y SHELL INCREMENTS  4 bytes.
.alias SINCY            $B4      ;[note] Shell Y velocity increment, 4 bytes: tank/robot pair as SHELLX (see SHELLX); SINCX's comment "X & Y SHELL INCREMENTS" describes this cell too  4 bytes.
.alias HITS             $B8      ;NUMBER OF HITS (SCORE)  4 bytes.
.alias RGOAL            $BC      ;TARGET ANGLE FOR ROBOT
.alias SXPCNT           $BD      ;SHELL EXPLOSION COUNTER
.alias RANGLE           $BE      ;RADAR ANGLE
.alias SANGLE           $BF      ;SAUCER ORIENTATION
.alias OTPOSX           $C0      ;OLD POSITIONS FOR TANKS TO BACF OUT OF COLL.  2 bytes.
.alias OTPOSY           $C2      ;[note] Saved old Y position for backing the tank/robot out of a collision (SAVEIT/BAKOUT), pairs with OTPOSX  2 bytes.
.alias ACTION           $C4      ;ROBOTS CURRENT DISPOSITION  [note] Robot behavior countdown: decremented once per frame (MAIN); 0 = decision time (ROBOT enters R_40S_ to pick a new behavior), otherwise counts down the frames left in the current one (e.g. TANKCK/R2D3CK set it to 1 for an immediate re-decision, ROBOT_5 sets $34 after backing out of a collision, R_EVAD sets $40 while evading).
.alias STATE            $C5      ;CALIFORNIA (HERE)  [note] Robot/buzz-bomber behavior-state flags, not indexed (there is only ever one active enemy): bit 0 = backing out of a collision or evading, bit 1 = which way to turn while doing so (meaningful only with bit 0 set), bit 7 = set by R_ATCK (attacking) / cleared by R_RAND (not attacking) - though nothing in this source re-tests bit 7 once set.  Atari's own comment, "CALIFORNIA (HERE)", is a joke (California = "here"), not a description.
.alias FRAME            $C6      ;FRAME COUNTER
.alias CRACK            $C7      ;CRACKED WINDSHIELD COUNTER
.alias OBJCOL           $C8      ;OBJECT COLLISION FLAG  [note] Also reached as OBJCOL+2 in BUZBOM (BZONE.MAC:3899-3906), the buzz bomber's twin of the tank's OBJCOL; lands on EIRNGE+1.
.alias EIRNGE           $C9      ;ENEMY IN RANGE FLAG  [note] Second byte (EIRNGE+1) is also reachable as OBJCOL+2, the buzz bomber's twin of the tank's OBJCOL (see OBJCOL).  2 bytes.
.alias R2D3FL           $CB      ;BUZZ BOMB FLAG
.alias LIVES            $CC      ;LIVE REMAINING
.alias GOVER            $CD      ;GAME OVER & HISCORE TABLE DISPLAY
.alias ATRACT           $CE      ;ATTRACT FLAG
.alias SKILL            $CF      ;SKILL OF PLAYER
.alias PTURN            $D0      ;AMT FOR PLAYER TO TURN
.alias FTIMER           $D1      ;TIMER FOR ALTERNATING ATTRACT STATES
.alias TIMOUT           $D2      ;TANK TIME OUT
.alias STIMER           $D3      ;SAUCER TIMER
.alias SCOLFG           $D4      ;SAUCER COLLISION FLAG
.alias SAPOSX           $D5      ;SAUCER POSITION  2 bytes.
.alias SAPOSY           $D7      ;[note] Saucer Y position (2 bytes); SAPOSX's comment "SAUCER POSITION" describes this cell too  2 bytes.
.alias SCANGL           $D9      ;SAUCER ANGLE
.alias SAINCX           $DA      ;SAUCCR MOTION INCREMENTS  2 bytes.
.alias SAINCY           $DC      ;[note] Saucer Y motion increment (2 bytes); SAINCX's comment "SAUCCR MOTION INCREMENTS" describes this cell too  2 bytes.
.alias SAUCER           $DE      ;SAUCER FLAG
.alias BOUNCE           $DF      ;COLLISION BOUNCE VIEW AMT
.alias S_CCTIM          $E0      ;COIN STUFF  [note] COIN65's per-mechanism (MECHS=3) electro-mechanical-counter pulse timer: sign bit set = pulse that counter on, clear = off (documented in TC65.MAC's own $CCTIM header comment).  3 bytes.
;1 unlabeled byte ($00E3): an anonymous '.BLKB 1' right after $CCTIM/EMCTRS (BZONE.MAC:277) - padding, never addressed by any instruction (see check_ramrefs.py).
.alias S_PSTSL          $E4      ;[note] COIN65's post-coin slam timers, one byte per coin mechanism (MECHS=3): a slam within this window invalidates the coin (documented in TC65.MAC's own header comments)  3 bytes.
.alias S_CNSTT          $E7      ;[note] COIN65's coin-status/detection timers, one byte per coin mechanism (MECHS=3): two counters packed in one byte for debounced coin-switch detection (documented in TC65.MAC's own header comments)  3 bytes.
.alias S_LMTIM          $EA      ;[note] COIN65's pre-coin slam-protection timer (1 byte): nonzero if the slam switch tripped within the last PRST*4 frames, which blocks coin input during that window (documented in TC65.MAC's own $LMTIM header comment)
.alias S_CMODE          $EB      ;[note] COIN65's coin-option-switch byte, copied from OPTON2 for the coin routine to read from zero page
.alias S_BCCNT          $EC      ;[note] COIN65's count of coins toward a bonus coin; cleared at power-on and game start
.alias VTIMER           $ED      ;VG TIMER  [note] Countdown timer, decremented once per NMI ("VG TIMER") and reloaded elsewhere; gates how often certain per-frame work runs.
.alias SOUNDN           $EE      ;SOUND # IN PROGRESS  [note] Declared by BZSOUN's own .BSECT layout ("SOUND # IN PROGRESS") but never referenced by name anywhere in this source; POINT/CURRENT/FRAMES/COUNT below are the per-channel sound state SNDON/MODSND actually read and write.
.alias POINT            $EF      ;POINTER OFFSET INTO DATA TABLES(IF 0, CHANNEL NOT ACTIVE)  4 bytes.
.alias CURRENT          $F3      ;OUTPUT VALUE  4 bytes.
.alias FRAMES           $F7      ;FRAMES UNTIL NEXT CHANGE  4 bytes.
.alias COUNT            $FB      ;COUNT OF TOTAL CHANGES  4 bytes.
;1 unlabeled byte ($00FF): BZSOUN's own .BSECT ends at COUNT ($FB-$FE, 4 bytes); nothing claims $FF.

;----------------------------------[ Game RAM ]----------------------------------
;$0100-$01FF: the 6502 hardware stack, not named RAM.  PWRON zeroes it along with the rest of RAM (BZSTST.MAC:96-100, 'STA $0100,X'), then STOP0 sets SP=$7F once RAMTST's zero-page shift-pattern test passes (BZSTST.MAC:232-233); SNDON also reads it directly, 'TSX; LDA $0103,X', to peek 3 bytes up its own just-pushed A/X/Y frame.
.alias PTBLX2           $0200    ;ROTATED & TRANSLATED OBJECT TABLE  56 bytes.
.alias PTBLY2           $0238    ;[note] Y coordinates of the rotated/translated object table (2 bytes per object), pairs with PTBLX2  56 bytes.
.alias PTBLO2           $0270    ;[note] Per-object type/orientation byte of the rotated/translated object table (PTBLX2/PTBLY2); read by DRAW/D_SBRT/D_SPCL to pick brightness and picture  56 bytes.
.alias EXPOSX           $02A8    ;EXPLOSION OBJECT TABLE  16 bytes.
.alias EXPOSY           $02B8    ;[note] Y coordinates of the explosion-piece table (2 bytes per piece), pairs with EXPOSX  16 bytes.
.alias EXPOSO           $02C8    ;[note] Per-piece orientation/spin byte for an exploding object, updated by the explosion physics loop alongside ZVELOC; copied to PTBLO2+1 to draw each piece via DRAW's D_SPCL/D_SBRT commands
.alias ZVELOC           $02C9    ;Z VELOCITY FOR EXPLOSION PIECES  15 bytes.
.alias EXPOSZ           $02D8    ;Z POSITION FOR EXPLOSION PIECES  16 bytes.
.alias TDIST            $02E8    ;[note] Distance (high byte) from the tank to the robot, computed by DRADAR (radar display) via the Mathbox square root; read by FIREIT/ROBOT and by BUZBOM for close-range checks
.alias BLIP             $02E9    ;BLIMP TIMER  [note] Radar-blip fade timer/brightness: DRADAR sets it to $F0 when the sweep passes an enemy ("SET BRIGHTNESS OF BLIP"), decrements it by 8 every frame after (DR_BLP), and also uses it directly (AND #$E0) as the blip's own draw intensity; reaching 0 stops the sweep beep and draw.  Atari's own comment, "BLIMP TIMER", is source shorthand for this radar "blip" timer - Battlezone has no blimp object.
.alias SINT             $02EA    ;SAUCER INTENSITY
.alias PL1MOV           $02EB    ;TEMP FOR POKEY SWITCH INPUT
.alias NOR2D3           $02EC    ;# OF R2D3'S -1 THIS GAME
.alias TRAP2            $02ED    ;SECURITY TRAP  [note] Security checksum, computed by MAIN ($510A-$511E) in attract mode only: EOR of the same $36 (54) bytes at offsets $02-$37 of the vector buffer being built, shifted right once.  Consumer: B_END_30 ($51C2: LDA TRAP2 / LSR / ORA POINT / BNE B_END_20) - only when TRAP2 is 0 or 1 and no sound is playing (POINT=0) does it go on to store 0 in SUPER, set LOX in SNDWRD and EXPCNT=$FF.  Independent of TRAP1.  2 bytes.
;17 unlabeled bytes ($02EF-$02FF) between TRAP2 and HSCTBL, never addressed by name; the last 2 ($02FD/$02FE) are the compile-time base of CKSCOR's 'HSCTBL-3,X'/'HSCTBL-2,X' shift-loop reads, but X is always >= 3 whenever those run (check_ramrefs.py), so even they are never actually read at runtime.
.alias HSCTBL           $0300    ;HIGH SCORES  30 bytes.
.alias INITLS           $031E    ;INITIALS  30 bytes.
.alias DSPLAY           $033C    ;ATTRACT DISPLAY STATE  [note] Attract-mode sub-state: alternates which attract-mode display is showing (the BATTLE ZONE logo animation vs. the high-score table), toggled once per attract cycle.
.alias STINTL           $033D    ;CURRENT INITIAL INPUT  3 bytes.
.alias SAVX             $0340    ;SCRATCH FOR X
.alias LTIMER           $0341    ;INITIAL ENTRY TIMOUT
.alias LETR             $0342    ;[note] Index (0-2) of the initials letter being entered on the high-score screen; advanced by the fire button (see LETCHK/HISCRE)
.alias OLSTAT           $0343    ;[note] Previous-frame ALLPOT switch reading, for edge-detecting the fire button while entering high-score initials (paired with NWSTAT)
.alias NWSTAT           $0344    ;SWITCH STATUSES  [note] Current-frame ALLPOT switch reading, captured for HISCRE/LETCHK's fire-button edge-detect against the previous frame's copy in OLSTAT (see OLSTAT).
.alias SUPER            $0345    ;SUPER BONUS FLAG
.alias LOGOX            $0346    ;LOGO POSITIONS  2 bytes.
.alias LOGOZ            $0348    ;[note] Z (depth) position of the 3-D attract-mode logo, 2 bytes ("LDA LOGOZ ;C SET UP Z", BZONE.MAC:490); pairs with LOGOX below, whose comment "LOGO POSITIONS" covers this cell too.  (Atari's comment here, "POKEY RANDOMNESS IMPROVEMENT", belongs to OLDRND below - see there.)  2 bytes.
.alias OLDRND           $034A    ;[note] Previous POKEY random byte, XORed into the next one in ROBCHK to make its dice roll less predictable
.alias LOGO             $034B    ;-1 IF DISPLAYING LOGO IN ATTRACT  2 bytes.
.alias OBJTIM           $034D    ;VOLCANO STUFF - SEE RUBIN  [note] Per-rock (NOROCK=5) active/lifetime countdown timer: 0 = rock inactive, nonzero counts down each frame (VOLCNO) toward re-triggering a new rock; also scaled (ASL x3, AND #$E0) as the rock's dot brightness when drawn (MOUNTS).  5 bytes.
.alias XSPD             $0352    ;[note] Per-rock (NOROCK=5) X speed, one byte per rock; part of the volcano rock-movement arrays with OBJTIM/YSPD/XPOSL/XPOSH/YPOSL/YPOSH (see VOLCNO)  5 bytes.
.alias YSPD             $0357    ;[note] Per-rock Y speed, one byte per rock (see XSPD)  5 bytes.
.alias XPOSL            $035C    ;[note] Low byte of each rock's X position, one byte per rock; XPOSH is the parallel high-byte array (positions are stored as two separate byte arrays, not interleaved words)  5 bytes.
.alias XPOSH            $0361    ;[note] High byte of each rock's X position, one byte per rock (see XPOSL)  5 bytes.
.alias YPOSL            $0366    ;[note] Low byte of each rock's Y position, one byte per rock (see XPOSL)  5 bytes.
.alias YPOSH            $036B    ;[note] High byte of each rock's Y position, one byte per rock (see XPOSL)  5 bytes.
.alias TRAP1            $0370    ;SOFTWARE SECURITY  [note] Security checksum, computed by BATTLE ($526C-$5284) in attract mode only (ATRACT bit 7 clear): 0 minus the $36 (54) bytes at offsets $02-$37 of the vector buffer being built (page = VGLIST+1 AND $F8), SBC-chained, minus OBJTBL+$21.  Consumers: NMI's START handler deducts it from the credits ($564D: SBC TRAP1 - so it must come out as exactly 1 for one credit per game); FINISH ($5588) stores it to VECRAM+0 during play once NOR2D3 >= 4.  Independent of TRAP2.
.alias YDISP            $0371    ;SCREEN YDISPLACEMENT - FOR HISCORE DISPLAY USE
;1166 unlabeled bytes ($0372-$07FF) through the end of RAM before the hardware registers at $0800 - YDISP ($0371) is the last cell this source ever names or addresses; nothing beyond it is ever read or written.

